  "Industrial Concentration of AI Infrastructure" — A Belief within skeptic discourse that the massive capital requirements for AI data centers consolidate control among a small number of hardware-owning firms.
Encompasses: proprietary bottlenecks, physical resource dependencies on power and water, capital expenditure barriers to entry, monopolization of compute infrastructure.
  ARGUE: cite specific data, peer-reviewed evidence, documented cases
  ATTACK VIA: counter-evidence, methodological critique, replication failure
  PREMISES: "The capital expenditure for building and maintaining frontier AI infrastructure (e.g., advanced data centers, specialized chips) is and will remain exceptionally high." — attackable; "These high capital requirements create significant and persistent barriers to entry for new firms or smaller entities." — attackable
  VULNERABLE: Rapid innovation in AI hardware and software, coupled with the rise of open-source models, cloud-agnostic solutions, or distributed computing paradigms, could decentralize compute power and reduce the strategic importance of owning physical infrastructure.
★ [skp-beliefs-017] (confidence: 0.62)
  "Compute and Energy Costs Impose Hard Ceilings on AI Scaling" — A Belief within skeptic discourse that identifies the financial limits at which AI development and deployment become economically unfeasible due to the high costs of computing power and energy. 
Encompasses: The specific financial thresholds where the expense of processing power and electricity makes AI projects unsustainable or impractical to scale.
  ARGUE: cite specific data, peer-reviewed evidence, documented cases
  ATTACK VIA: scope limits, competing frameworks, edge cases
